+llama: the encoder outputs of an encoder-decoder model live on the device (llama_memory_enc_dec, stage 2)
+
+Stage 2 of docs/notes/llama-cpp-encoder-decoder.md (Louise), on top of 0005.
+The per-sequence store is one F32 tensor in its own backend buffer,
+(n_seq_max + 1) regions of n_ubatch rows. llama_memory_i gains
+init_encode(ubatch), a virtual with a nullptr default: the encoder-decoder
+memory answers with the lowest free region, the T5 encoder graph writes
+its output rows there with ggml_set_rows, and llama_context::encode commits
+after the compute succeeded, so a failed encode keeps the old output. The
+decoder gathers the rows its ubatch's sequences own with ggml_get_rows on
+an I32 index input. The synchronize and memcpy of the encoder output and
+the per-step upload are gone; sequence operations move no data. State
+writes carry the rows through the io's tensor path (suffix version 2), so
+llama_memory_get_buffers sees the store and a single-sequence restore lands
+in a free region. No public API changes; only T5 reaches the new code.
+
+Checked on the CPU with the same tests as 0005: tests/test-llama-archs -a
+'^t5$' (a lone sequence bitwise equal to a one-sequence context; two in
+one batch within nmse 1e-4; removals, clear, seq_cp, a restored and a
+truncated state), tests/test-enc-dec-store rewritten for the device store
+(regions lowest-free and released by the commit, shared rows, the
+n_seq_max + 1 bound, state whole and per sequence, foreign files refused),
+and the 0004 server checks (a lone request byte-identical to 0003, the
+same 7 prompts in 3 orders, 60 requests after a burst, a late request
+answered in 0.06 s).
